&lt;h3&gt;


  The System Design Primer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If system design is something you're trying to learn, this is one resource I came across that is worth keeping around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;System Design Primer&lt;/strong&gt; is a free, open-source GitHub repository with a collection of system design topics, interview questions, real-world architectures, and other resources. It also has a study guide that gives you an idea of what to focus on depending on how much time you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It covers things like &lt;strong&gt;scalability, latency, availability, consistency, DNS, CDNs, load balancers, databases, caching, microservices, and distributed systems&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I liked here is that it also has actual system design questions to work through, such as &lt;strong&gt;Pastebin, Twitter, a web crawler, a social network, and scaling a system to millions of users on AWS&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a section on how to approach a system design interview, starting with requirements and constraints, then moving into the high-level design, core components, scaling, and trade-offs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So if you're just getting started with system design, or preparing for interviews and don't know what topics to go through first, this could be a good place to start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Format:&lt;/strong&gt; Free, open-source GitHub repository&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Covers:&lt;/strong&gt; System design, scalability, databases, caching, distributed systems, networking, microservices, and more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Also includes:&lt;/strong&gt; System design interview questions, real-world architectures, and Anki flashcards.&lt;/p&gt;

